        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "02d215bc-db82-5298-823e-097234bf16f1",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Nikolaas Tinbergen, Konrad Lorenz, William Homan Thorpe, Karl von Frisch, Patrick Bateson, Robert Hinde\n- Roles: biologist, ornithologist, zoologist, ethologist\n- Awards: Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine\n- Topics: animal behaviour, behavioural biology\n\nFacts:\n- Konrad Lorenz was born on 7 November 1903 and died on 27 February 1989.\n- Nikolaas Tinbergen was born on 15 April 1907 and died on 21 December 1988.\n- Konrad Lorenz was an Austrian zoologist, ethologist, and ornithologist.\n- Nikolaas Tinbergen was a Dutch biologist and ornithologist.\n- In 1973, Nikolaas Tinbergen shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with Karl von Frisch and Konrad Lorenz.\n- The 1973 Nobel Prize recognized discoveries by Nikolaas Tinbergen, Karl von Frisch, and Konrad Lorenz concerning the organisation and elicitation of individual and social behaviour patterns in animals.\n- William Homan Thorpe, together with Nikolaas Tinbergen, Patrick Bateson, and Robert Hinde, contributed to the growth and acceptance of behavioural biology in Great Britain.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "40a6c5ca-d708-5dfb-918d-ca139a75d705",
        "text": "Page: Nikolaas Tinbergen. Sentence: Nikolaas 'Niko' Tinbergen {'1': \", '2': \", '3': \", '4': \"} ( ; ] ; 15 April 1907 – 21 December 1988) was a Dutch biologist and ornithologist who shared the 1973 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with Karl von Frisch and Konrad Lorenz for their discoveries concerning organisation and elicitation of individual and social behaviour patterns in animals.\nPage: Konrad Lorenz. Sentence: Konrad Zacharias Lorenz (] ; 7 November 1903 – 27 February 1989) was an Austrian zoologist, ethologist, and ornithologist.\nPage: William Homan Thorpe. Sentence: Together with Nikolaas Tinbergen, Patrick Bateson and Robert Hinde, Thorpe contributed to the growth and acceptance of behavioural biology in Great Britain.",
